Chapter I – Familiar Face 

The console room was quiet. 

The rotor turned over slow and easy, and the roundels glowed their soft, patient glow, and the Doctor stood at the console with both hands flat on the edge of it, looking up at the ceiling. 

It went up a very long way. It always had. 

A smile found its way onto his face — not the enormous one, not the one that came out at the sight of a new sky. A smaller thing than that, and considerably heavier. 

"I don't want to do this alone," he said. 

He said it into the empty air, and the air, being the air of that particular ship, took it the way a good friend takes a confession: without fuss, and without pretending not to have heard. The lights along the gantry dipped. Just slightly. Just once. 

He pushed off from the console and walked, and his stride was light — lighter than it had any business being — the metal gangway ringing under his feet the same six notes it always rang, in the same order. 

The doors stood open at the end of it. 

Beyond them there was no ground, no city, no small furious person demanding to know what he thought he was playing at. There was only space, and a nebula sprawled across the whole of it in crimson and violet and great slow rivers of gold. 

He stopped in the doorway and set a hand against the frame. The wood was warm. It was always warm. 

"I'm never alone with you," he said, and the smile broadened, and for a moment there was nothing sad in it at all. 

Then the joy came up through him like weather. 

He shoved the doors wide with both hands and leaned out into the starlight, out over a drop that meant precisely nothing to him, and shouted it at the nebula and the stars and anyone at all who happened to be listening: 

"Joy to the world!" 

And he laughed. 

He stepped out onto the ledge of the police box — an ordinary painted step, made for milkmen and constables, presently sixteen thousand light-years from the nearest pavement — and the fire started in his hands. 

A prickle first, then a warmth, then that old familiar heat unspooling out of his wrists and pooling into his palms, curling round his fingers like something molten deciding to be affectionate about it. Golden. It was always so golden. 

He held his hands out in front of him and looked at them, blazing. 

"And that is exactly the word," he said. 

Then he looked back into the heart of his ship — one last time, and he knew it was the last time, and he let himself know it. His eyes were shining wet. His grin didn't waver by so much as a millimetre. 

"This has been," he said, "an absolute joy." 

Then he flung his arms wide to the cosmos, and let go. 

The light went out of him in a torrent, tearing out of his chest and his throat and his outstretched hands, shredding away into the dark. The fire took his silhouette and swallowed it whole, and inside the blaze his face began to come apart — softening, smoothing, the old lines dissolving into brightness. 

And then, as it always does, the fire went out. 

The last of it curled away in bright drifting motes, and standing where he had stood was a woman with blonde hair blazing in the nebula-light, blinking hard, both hands half-raised, wearing an expression of pure astonishment. 

He drew in a sharp breath. A grin broke over his face like sunrise over a hill. 

"Oh, hello!" he gasped. 

Then he was moving — back through the doors, back into the light of the console room, hands already up at his face. Jaw. Cheekbones. Mouth. The whole unfamiliar architecture of it, pressed and prodded and read like braille. Nose, present. Eyes, two of them, a good start. Hair — rather a lot of hair, and getting in the way. 

He stopped. 

Because it was familiar. 

Not familiar the way an old face is familiar, not one of his own worn a second time round with the corners rubbed off. Something else. Something he knew the shape of without being able to say from where. 

He stood very still in the middle of his ship, with both hands on a face that wasn't new at all, and waited for the name to arrive. 

The TARDIS gave him the mirror. 

It swung out of the wall on its little brass arm — the round shaving mirror that had followed her through six console rooms and had never once, in all that time, been asked for. She'd kept it. She'd been keeping it. 

He looked. 

And for a moment there was nothing, only a stranger looking back with his own startled expression on her face, and he thought: well, that's alright. New nose. Manageable hair. Could have been worse, could have had the ears again — 

And then it landed. 

It didn't arrive the way a name usually arrives, filed and fetched and handed over. It came the way a smell comes: all at once, sideways, dragging a whole decade in behind it. A council estate. A hand grabbing his in a basement full of shop dummies. Run. A beach in Norway with the wind taking every word that mattered. 

"Rose," he said. 

And it was her voice that said it. 

She heard it happen — heard the shape of the name in her own mouth, in that flat, warm, unmistakable London vowel — and her hand came up over it as though she could push the word back in and start again. 

"Rose Tyler," she said, to the mirror, to the ship, to nobody. "You've given me Rose Tyler." 

The rotor turned over slow and easy, and said nothing, and was not the least bit sorry. 

Because that was the question, and it arrived about four seconds after. 

Not what. She could live with what. She'd been young and old and tall and short and Scottish and, on one occasion she preferred not to revisit, briefly convinced she was a lorry driver from Rotherham. The what had never mattered very much. 

Why. 

Regeneration doesn't invent a face. That was the thing. Down in all that howling cellular chaos there is no workshop, no committee, nobody sketching cheekbones — it doesn't build, it fetches. It reaches back into Two thousand one hundred years of accumulated looking-at-people and it comes up holding a face, and once or twice in a very long life it has come up holding a face that already belonged to somebody. 

But those had been faces he'd seen. A man in Pompeii. A guard on Gallifrey. Strangers, in passing, filed and forgotten and then handed back with a message attached, if you were clever enough to read it. 

Rose Tyler was not a stranger. 

Rose Tyler was a living woman in a universe with a wall around it, sealed shut by his own hand, with a garden and a job and a man with one heart holding onto her — and you cannot take a face off somebody who is still using it. 

So either something had gone catastrophically wrong. 

Or something had gone extremely deliberately. 

And that was worse, because regeneration does not make mistakes. It makes choices. It always has. It simply has never, in all the centuries she'd been arguing with it, bothered to explain a single one. 

She leaned in close to the little brass mirror until her breath fogged it. 

"Why you?" she said. "Out of everything. Out of everyone. Why you, and why now?" 

The face in the mirror didn't know either. 

That was the worst of it, really. It looked just as frightened as she was. 

The Doctor sensed it before he understood it.

A scent.

Regeneration had sharpened his senses, but this fragrance seemed to reach beyond the physical world. It was familiar, yet filled with a longing she couldn't explain. It felt as though it had accompanied her since childhood, remaining close without ever demanding her attention.

There was something unusual about it. Something that carried joy, sorrow, yearning, fear, and emotions that had no names.

Every scent was a key to a memory. Love. Hatred. Happiness. Days erased from conversation but never from the mind.

What memory did this scent unlock for the Doctor?

And why did it feel so important?

Before she could find out, her eyes closed.

A corridor appeared.

Long. Narrow. Dark.

A passage that seemed to stretch beyond the limits of the space it occupied.

Pain spread through her head. Her skull felt ready to split. She thought it might be the regeneration, until she remembered the impact against the console.

She had fallen.

And now she was dreaming.

But Time Lords were not supposed to dream like this.

Why was she here?

She activated her sonic screwdriver and used its light to examine the walls.

The first face.

The second.

Three. Four. Five.

Her former incarnations.

The faces of the lives she had already lived, lined up along the corridor as though time itself had preserved them.

Ten.

Eleven.

Half of twelve.

Then a voice spoke behind her.

She recognised it, though its position made no sense. Only minutes earlier, she had heard that familiar London accent. But now it came from behind her, from somewhere outside her own voice.

"Hello."

She turned.

Rose.

"Why you?" the Doctor asked. "Why did I choose you?"

They sounded identical. Same tone. Same accent. Two voices that should not have belonged to the same moment.

She couldn't tell whether the figure behind her was Rose or another reflection of herself.

"You didn't choose me."

Rose pointed her sonic screwdriver at her.

"I chose you."

Her two hearts began to pound with terrifying speed.

Then the headache eased. Her eyes opened.

She was back beside the TARDIS console.

Asleep.

Time Lords rarely experienced dreams. Their minds were not meant to wander through the same unconscious landscapes as those of other species. Unless, of course, something had interfered.

Her hearts beat in a strange pattern: four rapid pulses, like knocks upon a door.

Knock. Knock. Knock. Knock.

She forced her hearts back under control.

Then she checked the colour of her kidneys.

She stopped.

"Why can't I get the colour right?"

It was an absurd question, and she knew it. Yet her newly regenerated mind seemed incapable of settling on anything for long.

The dream mattered more.

She had dreamed only minutes after regeneration, and the words continued to echo through her thoughts.

"You didn't choose me. I chose you."

She searched for meaning in the sentence. It offered her none.

A new body. A new mind. An unfamiliar pattern of thoughts. And now a dream that felt less like a dream and more like a message.

She was exhausted.

Then the TARDIS sounded an alarm.

A sharp, explosive bang echoed through the chamber. The ship began to shake violently.

The Doctor seized the console.

"Ahhh! Classic."

The tablet displayed a warning.

WARNING: TARDIS REGENERATING. NEAREST LANDING SITE SELECTED.

She tried to stabilise the ship, but the knowledge she needed seemed to slip away from her.

"Reduce the power. Keep the control rod balanced."

She paused.

"That's what I'm supposed to do, isn't it?"

She wasn't sure.

The TARDIS began to collapse. Glass spheres shattered, and the columns came crashing down.

She needed to act, but the answer eluded her.

"River would know what to do."

She struck a red button.

Ketchup burst from beneath the console.

She stared at it.

"Apparently, I've been very bored."

She turned a valve-like knob.

"PARTY MODE ACTIVATED."

Confetti filled the air.

The Doctor looked around, utterly unimpressed.

"That's not going to help."

She found a covered button.

"Let's hope I've installed something useful."

She lifted the cover and pressed it.

The outer doors opened.

Air pressure changed instantly. A powerful current tore through the chamber, forcing her to cling to the console. The TARDIS burned and crumbled around her, racing towards its selected destination at maximum speed.

She reached for the door control.

"Come on. Come on!"

At the last possible moment, her fingertips found the button.

She pressed it.

The doors shut.

A notification sounded.

She struggled towards the tablet.

"Collision imminent. Ten... nine... eight..."

She braced herself.

"Three. Two. One."

Silence.

The corridor.

Again.

The Doctor took out her sonic screwdriver again.

This time, the walls showed someone else. A woman of average height, with blonde hair. It wasn't Rose, of course. It was someone else entirely, someone the Doctor didn't recognise.

There were several pictures of her, one after another, stretching along the walls. The Doctor kept walking down the corridor. For what felt like an eternity, there was nothing but the endless passageway and the countless images of this mysterious woman.

Eventually, she reached the end.

There, carved into the wall, was a name.

“LAUROLEI.”

Then, once again, a voice came from behind her.

But this time, it didn't sound like a woman's voice. It was higher, younger.

The Doctor turned around.

A child stood before her, dressed in worn-out clothes, holding a leaf in one hand. The child stared straight at her.

“Why did you forget me?”

The Doctor couldn't make sense of it. And yet, there was something strangely familiar about the child, as though she recognised them from a memory buried deep in her distant past.

“Who are you?”

“The child you left in the middle of hell! WHY DID YOU FORGET ME?”

The moment the child screamed, the entire place began to collapse. The walls crumbled, and the floor started disappearing beneath the Doctor's feet.

The child kept screaming.

“Why? WHY? WHY?”

With every cry, more of the walls fell apart, and more of the floor vanished.

At last, the Doctor could see what lay beyond the walls.

It looked like hell itself. A blood-red sky, thunderous lightning, and dark, swirling clouds.

“Oh, my God,” the Doctor whispered. “Where is this place?”

And suddenly, the Doctor felt as though her hearts were about to stop beating.

Because although there were very few moments when the Doctor was truly afraid, there were still things that could frighten her, of course. Places like the one she had just seen.

The ground beneath her was strange, and it tasted absolutely disgusting.

Her hearts were still racing.

Knock, knock, knock, knock.

That rhythm was beginning to irritate her. Her hearing was so sensitive now that she could actually hear the beating of her own hearts from inside her chest.

Slowly, she opened her eyes.

This wasn't the inside of the TARDIS.

Apparently, during the collision, the TARDIS had somehow thrown her outside.

The Doctor slowly got to her feet, brushed the dirt off her clothes, and turned around.

The TARDIS was right beside her.

She tried the door.

Locked.

It seemed the TARDIS was still regenerating.

Well, there was only one thing left to do. She would have to begin her first adventure with this familiar face.

“Let's see where the universe has brought us this time.”

 

 
